# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Nightly restart helper  -- paired with doorbell-restart.timer
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS:
#   The exhibit runs every day, unattended, for months. Long-lived processes
#   accumulate problems you will never have time to hunt down -- a slow memory
#   leak, a DRM buffer that isn't quite freed on the thousandth preview cycle,
#   a camera pipeline that gets slightly wedged. Restarting once a day before
#   the museum opens costs 2 seconds and makes all of those irrelevant.
#
#   This is standard practice for unattended installations. It is not an
#   admission that the code is buggy; it is refusing to bet the exhibit on
#   the code being perfect for 90 days straight.
#
# WHY A TIMER AND NOT 'RuntimeMaxSec=1d' IN doorbell.service:
#   RuntimeMaxSec restarts 24h after the service last started, which DRIFTS.
#   Whatever time it fires today, it fires a bit later tomorrow, and sooner or
#   later it lands at 2pm on a Saturday in the middle of a kid's turn.
#   A timer fires at a wall-clock time we choose: before opening, every day.

[Unit]
Description=Restart the doorbell exhibit

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# ┗━━ Runs one command and exits. There is no long-running process here --
#     this unit exists only to be triggered by the timer.

ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl restart doorbell.service
